---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { ResizerTool } from "../../../components/image/ResizerTool.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/image/resizer")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Resize a PNG, JPG, or WebP image to a target width and/or height, optionally keeping its original aspect ratio."
>
  <ResizerTool client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Enter a target width, height, or both. With "Lock aspect ratio" checked and only one dimension entered, the
      other is calculated automatically; with both entered, the image is scaled to fit inside that box without
      distortion. Unchecking the lock stretches the image to the exact dimensions you enter.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 1600×900 image resized to a width of 800 with aspect ratio locked produces an 800×450 output. The same image
      resized to exactly 800×800 with the lock unchecked produces an 800×800 output that is stretched vertically.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Output dimensions are capped (currently 10,000px per side) to avoid excessive memory use.</li>
      <li>Resizing up (enlarging) is supported but can make an image look soft or blurry, since no new detail is created.</li>
      <li>Choosing JPEG output flattens transparency onto your chosen background color; PNG and WebP preserve it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Accepted inputs</h2>
    <p>
      One PNG, JPG, or WebP, up to 25 MB and about 40 million pixels. Width and height are limited to 10,000 pixels. The form opens with width 800, height blank, aspect ratio locked, and PNG output. A 1600×900 image at those settings becomes 800×450.
    </p>

    <h2>When the file leaves your device</h2>
    <p>
      Choosing a file and seeing the preview keeps it on your device. It stays there while "Process on your device (default)" is selected. The file is sent only if you select "Upload to our server for processing" and then press Resize. The server holds that file in memory for the one request and does not write it to disk. A failure on your device does not upload the file by itself.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
